# No Purple, No Gradients

Two rules for components and pages:

1. Do not use purple, violet, or fuchsia anywhere — solid colors, borders, text, or otherwise.
2. Do not use gradients at all, of any color. Use flat, solid colors only.

This applies to Tailwind utility classes, arbitrary values, inline styles, and CSS.

Stick to the approved palette defined in `web/src/app/globals.css` (`--color-brand`, `--color-ink`, `--color-paper`, `--color-slate`, `--color-amber`, `--color-success`) or their Tailwind equivalents (`brand`, `ink`, `paper`, `slate`, `amber`, `success`).

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — purple/violet/fuchsia utility classes
<div className="bg-purple-500 text-violet-600">...</div>

// ❌ BAD — any gradient, even non-purple
<div className="bg-gradient-to-r from-brand to-brand-dark">...</div>

// ❌ BAD — arbitrary hex/hsl values that are purple
<div className="bg-[#8B5CF6]" style={{ color: "#a855f7" }}>...</div>

// ✅ GOOD — flat, solid color from the approved palette
<div className="bg-brand text-paper">...</div>
```

```css
/* ❌ BAD — gradient, and purple */
.badge {
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, #8b5cf6, #d946ef);
}

/* ✅ GOOD — flat color */
.badge {
  background: var(--color-brand);
}
```

If a design calls for an accent beyond the current palette, propose a new non-purple, non-gradient color token in `globals.css` instead.
